.TH CLRU_DESTROY 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clru_destroy \- destroy an LRU cache and invalidate the handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clrucache.h>

clru_destroy(name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Waits until every in-flight resolved use of the handle stops, then
evicts all the entries that remain, calling the eviction callback for
each one while it holds the lock of the owning segment. It then
destroys the map of every segment, frees all the memory, and sets
name to CLRU_CACHE_INVALID.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  The cache variable to destroy. The macro sets it to
      CLRU_CACHE_INVALID after the destruction
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A call is safe when name is CLRU_CACHE_INVALID, and it does nothing.
A call on a stale handle is a FATAL ERROR: it calls abort() and raises
SIGABRT, and is never a silent double-free. A handle is stale when an
earlier, sequential call destroyed it, or when another thread destroys
it at the same time. A forged value and garbage are fatal errors too:
the handle carries a generation tag, which the library checks before
it touches the cache behind the handle, so the library always detects
such a value and never dereferences it.
A concurrent, ordinary call to clrucache_get_full(),
clrucache_set_full(), clrucache_size() or clrucache_capacity() on the
same handle is not misuse; this call waits for that call to finish
before it frees anything.

The macro evaluates name exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array, so an expression with a
side effect, such as handles[i++], destroys and clears one element.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clru_construct (3),
.BR clrucache_create_full (3)
